---
title: Local sandbox
description: Run agent tools inside an operating-system sandbox and see what it allows before launch.
order: 5
---

# Local sandbox

Agent tools run with your normal access unless you choose a sandbox. Open a
workspace's **Agent sandbox** view to set the default for new agent sessions. A
session can override that default. The sandbox applies to tools the agent calls
and to their child processes. Terminals and editors that Pau opens stay
unsandboxed unless you open a [sandboxed terminal](#terminals).

When you choose a sandbox, Pau shows the effective access for Pi or Claude
before any tool runs. If the sandbox cannot start, tool calls fail. Pau never
runs them outside the sandbox instead.

## Requirements

On Linux, Pau uses Bubblewrap, `socat`, and ripgrep. Install `bwrap` 0.11 or
later, `socat`, and `rg`, then restart Pau. Pau looks them up once when it
starts and uses those exact files for every sandboxed launch, so a directory
added to `PATH` later cannot replace them. If one is missing, the view names it
and the sandbox choice stays disabled.

On macOS, Pau uses Seatbelt. Pau's own checks have not yet run on macOS, and the
effective access says so.

## Profiles

Both profiles block network access and hide credential stores until you grant
more.

- **Workspace write** allows writes to the workspace and a temporary directory
  for the session.
- **Read only** allows writes only to the session's temporary directory.

Every profile denies reads of common credential stores, such as `~/.ssh`,
`~/.aws`, `~/.config/gh`, Pi's `auth.json`, and Claude's credentials file. It
also denies the workspace's `.env`, `.env.local`, and `.env.*.local` files.
`.env.example` stays readable.

Writes to repository and agent configuration are blocked even inside the
workspace: `.git/hooks`, `.git/config`, `.pi`, `.claude`, `.codex`,
`.mcp.json`, `.vscode`, `.idea`, `.zed`, and shell startup files. A sandboxed
tool therefore cannot plant configuration that an unsandboxed agent or editor
would load later.

Only `HOME`, `LANG`, `PATH`, `TERM`, `TMPDIR`, `PAU_SANDBOX`, and configured
credential source names reach sandboxed tools. Each credential source contains
a sentinel instead of the real value. Provider keys and other variables in
Pau's environment do not. Core dumps are disabled, and child processes stop
when the sandbox ends. Pau does not cap CPU, memory, or process count.

## Grants

Each grant widens the profile, and the effective access lists it.

- **Network destinations** take a host, a wildcard such as `*.example.com`, or a
  host and port such as `127.0.0.1:5173`. Requests go through a proxy that
  admits only these destinations. A single-label name such as `intranet` is not
  accepted.
- **Extra writable directories** take absolute paths. A directory that contains
  your home directory or a credential store is refused.
- **Allow Unix sockets** allows every socket, because Linux cannot limit sockets
  by path. That includes the Docker and SSH agent sockets.
- **Block reads from the home directory** hides your home directory except the
  workspace and the files the sandbox needs to start. Turn it on when you grant
  a network destination, because any readable file could then leave the
  computer.
- **Share /tmp/claude with Claude Code** keeps that directory writable. Pau
  blocks it by default because every sandbox that shares it can change the
  others' temporary files.

Network grants apply to a running session. A change to files or directories
waits for the current tool call to finish, then restarts the sandbox before
the next call.

## Credential references

A sandbox profile can save references to credentials in Pau's host environment.
Each reference contains an environment variable name, such as `PACKAGE_TOKEN`,
and the TLS destinations that may receive it. Enter each destination as a host,
wildcard, or host and port without a URL scheme or path.

Pau stores the variable name and destinations, never the variable's value. Model
provider credentials cannot be referenced. For each session, the tool
environment receives a unique sentinel under the source variable's name. Pau
sends the real value only to a dedicated broker process through a private pipe,
keeps it out of process arguments and logs, and clears the broker mapping when
the session ends.

Pau substitutes a sentinel in request headers or bodies only when the request
uses the sandbox's TLS-terminating proxy and its host and port match that
credential's destination list. The proxy verifies the upstream certificate
before it sends the real value. Plain HTTP, SOCKS-only traffic, excluded TLS
hosts, certificate-pinned clients, and mTLS connections receive the sentinel
or fail instead. Pau does not retry the request outside the sandbox.

A destination on the list can receive the real credential when a sandboxed
tool sends its sentinel. The destination can use that credential on your behalf
or return it in a response, where the sandboxed tool can read it. Grant only
destinations you trust with the credential and the operations it authorizes.

## Pi

Pi keeps its model credentials and provider traffic outside the sandbox. Pau
loads an extension that runs Pi's `read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`,
`find`, and `ls` tools, and `!` commands, inside it. When grep cannot read a
file because of the sandbox, its result lists the skipped files so you and the
model know the search was incomplete.

Tools from other Pi extensions are disabled. To allow one, add its name under
**Tools allowed outside the sandbox**. Each call then asks for your approval and
runs with your normal access.

With `pi-mcp-adapter` installed, Pau gives the session its own MCP list:

- A stdio server starts inside the sandbox with the same restrictions as every
  tool.
- A remote server is included only when its host is a granted network
  destination. Pi's MCP client still connects from the Pi process, not through
  the sandbox proxy.
- Other servers are disabled for the session.

## Claude

Pau starts Claude Code with its own sandbox and the same profile. Claude's
sandbox covers its Bash tool and the commands it starts, and it cannot retry a
blocked command outside the sandbox. Claude's Read, Edit, and Write tools follow
Claude's permission rules rather than the operating-system sandbox. `WebFetch`
and `WebSearch` are denied unless you allow them outside the sandbox, which
makes Claude ask before each call. Claude's MCP servers are disabled because
Claude would start them outside its sandbox.

## Terminals

A sandboxed terminal runs your shell under the workspace's sandbox profile, the
one new sessions use. Open one with the shield button next to **New terminal**.
Its tab shows the profile's name. Terminals opened with **New terminal** keep
your normal access.

The shell gets the profile's file, network, and environment limits, with `TERM`
set to `xterm-256color` and `SHELL` set to your shell. Job control, Ctrl-C,
Ctrl-Z, resizing, and terminal editors such as Neovim work as usual.

Programs in the sandbox cannot open your other terminals, so they cannot read
what you type there or type into them. Keystrokes that a program injects into
its own terminal reach only programs in the same sandbox. When the shell exits
or you close the terminal, Pau stops every process left in the terminal's
session, including jobs started with `nohup`.

Some things differ from a sandboxed session:

- Credential references do not apply. Their variables are not set in the
  terminal.
- Your shell cannot save its history or other files outside the writable
  directories.
- On macOS, a program that starts its own session, as a daemon does, keeps
  running after the terminal closes.

Sandboxed terminals need sandbox-runtime's `srt` command. Install it with
`npm install --global @anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime@0.0.77`, then restart Pau.
Until then, the shield button is disabled and its tooltip says why. If a
sandboxed terminal fails to start, the button stays disabled until the
workspace's sandbox settings change. Pau does not open an unsandboxed shell
instead.

## Denials

The session transcript shows each sandbox denial where it occurred. A file
denial names the operation and path. A network denial names the destination.
If Pau can tell that the sandbox may have caused a failure but cannot identify
the blocked access, the transcript labels it as an unexplained sandboxed
failure. Open the failed tool call to read its error.

Change the active sandbox policy before retrying an operation that it blocks.
Permission approval does not override the sandbox.

## Placeholder files

To block creating a protected path that does not exist yet, the sandbox puts an
empty file or directory there, such as `.mcp.json` or `.claude`. Pau lists
these placeholders before launch and hides them from file and Git views while
the sandbox runs. They are removed when the session ends. If Pau stops
unexpectedly, it removes any that are still empty before the next sandboxed
launch. A placeholder you have since written to is left alone.

## Permission prompts

Permission prompts decide when an agent asks before acting. They are separate
from the sandbox and never widen what it allows.
